input: tca8418: service the chip on resume

The driver has no PM callbacks, so the chip is never looked at across a
system suspend.  On a board that wires INT to an edge-triggered line, as
the Pyra does, that loses the keypad for good whenever a key changes
state while the system is asleep.

The key event is queued and INT is asserted, but the falling edge
happens while the gpio bank is not latching edges for a line that is not
a wakeup source.  Nothing marks the interrupt pending, so nothing replays
it at resume.  The chip goes on holding INT low, setting a status bit
that is already set produces no new edge, and the handler is never
entered again.

Seen on a Pyra V5.3 with the in-handler loop already applied: hold a
key, suspend with an RTC wake, release the key while asleep.  After the
resume REG_INT_STAT reads 0x01, REG_KEY_LCK_EC reads 0x0a (the FIFO
full), the interrupt count in /proc/interrupts is frozen and the
keyboard is dead.  Writing REG_INT_STAT by hand over i2c brings it back.

Service the chip in a resume callback, with the interrupt disabled so
that a handler thread already running cannot read the FIFO at the same
time; disable_irq() waits for it.  The events queued while asleep are
reported, so a key released during the suspend is reported released
rather than left held.  Probe never set the client data, so set it for
the callback to find.

--- a/drivers/input/keyboard/tca8418_keypad.c
+++ b/drivers/input/keyboard/tca8418_keypad.c
@@ -338,6 +338,7 @@ static int tca8418_keypad_probe(struct i2c_client *client,
 
 	keypad_data->client = client;
 	keypad_data->row_shift = row_shift;
+	i2c_set_clientdata(client, keypad_data);
 
 	/* Read key lock register, if this fails assume device not present */
 	error = tca8418_read_byte(keypad_data, REG_KEY_LCK_EC, &reg);
@@ -402,6 +403,31 @@ static int tca8418_keypad_probe(struct i2c_client *client,
 	return 0;
 }
 
+static int __maybe_unused tca8418_resume(struct device *dev)
+{
+	struct i2c_client *client = to_i2c_client(dev);
+	struct tca8418_keypad *keypad_data = i2c_get_clientdata(client);
+
+	/*
+	 * A key that changes state while the system is suspended queues an
+	 * event and asserts INT, but the falling edge can come while the gpio
+	 * bank is not latching edges for a line that is not a wakeup source,
+	 * so nothing marks the interrupt pending and nothing replays it.  The
+	 * pin then stays asserted and, as in tca8418_service(), no later edge
+	 * can arrive.  Service the chip now so the pin starts released.
+	 *
+	 * disable_irq() waits for a handler thread that is already running,
+	 * so the two never read the FIFO at the same time.
+	 */
+	disable_irq(client->irq);
+	tca8418_service(keypad_data);
+	enable_irq(client->irq);
+
+	return 0;
+}
+
+static SIMPLE_DEV_PM_OPS(tca8418_pm_ops, NULL, tca8418_resume);
+
 static const struct i2c_device_id tca8418_id[] = {
 	{ "tca8418", 8418, },
 	{ }
@@ -418,6 +444,7 @@ static struct i2c_driver tca8418_keypad_driver = {
 	.driver = {
 		.name	= "tca8418_keypad",
 		.of_match_table = tca8418_dt_ids,
+		.pm	= &tca8418_pm_ops,
 	},
 	.probe		= tca8418_keypad_probe,
 	.id_table	= tca8418_id,
